{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg8pkTUFS5dASZi7C8cEi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WYt3A2fnXnvbuZr2vdnFXU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/nick/code/webtorrent-desktop.memory-leaks-05-file-selection-applies-only-changes; ls src/engine; cat src/engine/AGENTS.md | head -150; grep -n \"select\\|torrentClose\\|function startTorrenting\" src/engine/index.js; cat test/unit/engine-torrent-close-wiring.js; cat package.json | grep -n '\"test\\|\"lint'"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4718,"cache_read_input_tokens":41278,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4718},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f96e20b8-4586-4652-a1ec-e1f2211c71ab","uuid":"f95b8a55-5a93-4610-9f20-5eaa59c07ae6","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:20:19.363Z","request_id":"req_011Cg8pkTBdqmHT8z6fawfzB","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01WYt3A2fnXnvbuZr2vdnFXU":{"command":"cd /Users/nick/code/webtorrent-desktop.memory-leaks-05-file-selection-applies-only-changes; ls src/engine; cat src/engine/AGENTS.md | head -150; grep -n \"select\\|torrentClose\\|function startTorrenting\" src/engine/index.js; cat test/unit/engine-torrent-close-wiring.js; cat package.json | grep -n '\"test\\|\"lint'"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WYt3A2fnXnvbuZr2vdnFXU","type":"tool_result","content":"AGENTS.md\nannounce.js\naudio-metadata.js\nbus-websocket.js\ndials.js\nhashfail.js\nindex.js\nstall.js\n# src/engine/\n\nThe torrent engine and its transport (ADR-012). Phase 3 moves the\nengine here from `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` and turns it into a Node\nsidecar; US-039 landed the transport seam first and US-040 added the\nsidecar entry point.\n\n**There is exactly one engine.** US-044 moved the UI onto the sidecar\nand US-045 deleted the hidden window, the engine that ran in it and the\n`ipcMain` relay between them. Engine behaviour has a single home now:\n`src/engine/index.js`.\n\n`build:main` compiles the whole of `src` except the four Vite-owned\npaths, so a file added here is emitted to `build/engine/` with no build\nchange -- that is also the path unit tests require.\n\n## The bus contract\n\nThe engine talks to the UI through an injected bus and never names a\ntransport API itself. A bus is exactly two functions:\n\n- `send(channel, ...args)` -- deliver one message to the UI.\n- `on(channel, handler)` -- subscribe; resolves to an unsubscribe\n  function. The handler receives the protocol's argument list only,\n  never the transport's own event object. Stripping that object is the\n  bus's job, not the caller's.\n\nThe bus carries no protocol. Channel names, argument lists and payload\nshapes live in the command and event tables in\n`docs/platform-shim.md`; the codec that turns them into bytes is\n`src/shared/wire.js`. A bus implementation that knows a channel name is\na bug.\n\nThe engine constructs its bus in exactly one place and\n`test/unit/engine-entry.js` asserts that, by scanning the engine source\nfor transport APIs, for stray `.send(` calls and for subscriptions that\nbypass `bus.on`. That scan is what keeps the seam substitutable, so keep\nit passing rather than relaxing it.\n\n`bus-electron.js`, the Phase 3 transitional implementation, is gone:\nUS-044 pointed the UI at `bus-websocket.js` and US-045 deleted the\nhidden window it depended on, along with its unit test.\n\nThis directory is outside `src/renderer/`, so the renderer's Node bans\ndo not apply: the sidecar is a Node process and uses `fs`, `crypto`\nand friends directly. Since US-046 both boundary mechanisms cover it\nfor the **opposite** mistake instead. `eslint-boundary.config.js` has\nan `src/engine/**` override banning `electron`, `@electron/remote` and\n`original-fs` plus every DOM global, and `npm run lint:boundary` lints\nthis tree as well as the renderer;\n`test/unit/platform-boundary.js` scans the same two things in source,\nwhich is what catches the `require()` spellings ESLint cannot see.\n\n## `bus-websocket.js`\n\nThe sidecar implementation, and the one that survives Phase 4. It binds\na `ws` server to `127.0.0.1` on port 0, encodes every frame with\n`src/shared/wire.js`, and exposes `send`/`on` plus three lifecycle\nfunctions the entry point needs: `listen()` (resolves to the bound\nport, idempotent), `address()` and `close()`.\n\nThree behaviours are contract, not incidental:\n\n- **A send with nobody connected is dropped, not queued.** Commands\n  always originate from the UI, so a connected client is the normal\n  case; the pre-connect queue that ADR-012 requires lives on the *UI*\n  side, in `ipc-engine.js`, and has since US-044.\n- **A malformed frame and a throwing handler are both reported through\n  `onError`, never rethrown and never fatal to the socket.** The engine\n  is a long-lived process; one bad message from a buggy UI must not end\n  the session. `index.js` wires `onError` to the same\n  `wt-uncaught-error` path the process handlers use.\n- **The token, when configured, is checked in `verifyClient`**, which\n  answers a bad upgrade with HTTP 401 before any frame is read. It is\n  read from `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_TOKEN` by `index.js`. No token means an\n  open server, which is only acceptable for running the sidecar by\n  hand. The `Origin` check ADR-012 also asks for is still not\n  implemented -- US-044 connected the UI without needing it, so it needs\n  the real header the Electron and Tauri webviews send and is US-046\n  territory.\n\n## `index.js`\n\nThe sidecar entry point: `node build/engine/index.js`. It imports no\nElectron module and uses no DOM.\n\n**The stdout contract with the host.** Exactly one line matches\n`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=<port>`, printed once the server is listening.\nThe host end is `src-tauri/src/engine.rs`, which holds the same prefix\nin `PORT_LINE_PREFIX`; keep the prefix and the `=` if you touch either\nside. Every other line on stdout and stderr is pumped through to the\nhost's own output under an `Engine: ` prefix. `src/main/` is gone, so\nthe Electron reader that older plans name here\n(`src/main/engine-process.js`, US-041) went with it.\n\nDifferences from the hidden-window engine, all deliberate:\n\n- No `ipcReadyWebTorrent`. Readiness is the port line plus an accepted\n  socket.\n- No `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and no `wt-poster`. Both needed a DOM;\n  US-043 deleted them from the wire and poster generation now runs in the\n  UI renderer (`src/renderer/lib/torrent-poster.js`, ADR-013). The engine\n  handles no media at all -- do not add a media dependency back to it.\n- No `window.client` and no `window.testOfflineMode`.\n- Uncaught errors come from `process.on('uncaughtException')` and\n  `process.on('unhandledRejection')` rather than a `window` error\n  listener, and still go out on `wt-uncaught-error`.\n- `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT` destroy the client and close every streaming\n  server, so a quit does not leave the download directory held open.\n- With `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE` set, stdin ending shuts the\n  process down the same way. That is the orphan backstop for a host\n  that dies without running its `before-quit`, and it is gated behind\n  the flag because a hand-run `node build/engine/index.js` gets\n  `/dev/null` on stdin, which ends immediately.\n- `getTorrent()` throws a plain `Error` with the same message\n  `TorrentKeyNotFoundError` produced. The class lives in\n  `src/renderer/lib/errors.js`, only its message ever crossed the wire,\n  and the sidecar does not import renderer code.\n\nThe streaming servers are a `Map` keyed by info hash, and each entry\ncounts its holders (US-043): `wt-start-server` is idempotent per torrent\nand always replies, `wt-stop-server` takes the info hash to release, and\nthe server dies with its last holder. Poster generation and playback can\nhold servers for different torrents at once, so a single module-level\n`server` is not enough any more.\n\n`config.TORRENT_PATH` and `config.POSTER_PATH` resolve under plain\n`node` because US-040 made `src/config.js` require `electron` lazily,\ninside the main-process branch of `getPath()`. Do not hoist that back\nto a top-level import: the packaged sidecar has no\n`node_modules/electron` to resolve.\n\nThe host runs the sidecar as **Electron in Node mode**\n(`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1`), not as a separate `node` binary, so that the\npackaged app needs no second runtime. In that mode\n`process.versions.electron` is set but `process.type` is not and\n`require('electron')` has no `app`, which is why `getPath()` in\n`src/config.js` branches on `process.type` and treats anything that is\nneither `'renderer'` nor `'browser'` as a plain Node process. A check\nfor \"is Electron present\" there breaks the sidecar at load, and only\noutside `NODE_ENV=test` (portable config skips the branch).\n\n## Per-torrent state and the torrent's `close`\n\nState the engine keys by info hash (`lastAnnounceAt`,\n`corruptPeerStats`, the stall tracker) is forgotten in one place:\n`torrentClose`, the `close` handler `addTorrentEvents` installs.\nWebTorrent emits `close` from `destroy()` whatever the cause --\n`wt-stop-torrenting`, removal, a fatal error -- so `stopTorrenting`\nonly destroys and does not repeat the cleanup. Add new per-torrent\n134:  bus.on('wt-start-torrenting', (torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections) =>\n135:    startTorrenting(torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections))\n148:  bus.on('wt-select-files', (infoHash, selections) =>\n149:    selectFiles(infoHash, selections))\n222:function startTorrenting (torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections) {\n235:  torrent.once('ready', () => selectFiles(torrent, selections))\n240:  // The per-torrent state goes in `torrentClose`, which `destroy()`\n329:  torrent.once('close', torrentClose)\n343:  function torrentClose () {\n672:function selectFiles (torrentOrInfoHash, selections) {\n681:    throw new Error('selectFiles: missing torrent ' + torrentOrInfoHash)\n686:  // selection with individual selections for each file, so we can\n687:  // select/deselect files later on\n688:  if (!selections) {\n689:    selections = new Array(torrent.files.length).fill(true)\n693:  if (selections.length !== torrent.files.length) {\n694:    throw new Error('got ' + selections.length + ' file selections, ' +\n698:  // Remove default selection (whole torrent)\n699:  torrent.deselect(0, torrent.pieces.length - 1, false)\n701:  // Add selections (individual files)\n702:  selections.forEach((selection, i) => {\n704:    if (selection) {\n705:      file.select()\n707:      console.log('deselecting file ' + i + ' of torrent ' + torrent.name)\n708:      file.deselect()\nconst test = require('tape')\nconst fs = require('fs')\nconst path = require('path')\n\n// The engine keeps per-torrent diagnostic state -- announce times,\n// corrupt-peer tallies, stall samples -- keyed by info hash. A torrent\n// can close without `wt-stop-torrenting` (a fatal error, say), and\n// state left behind under its info hash greets the torrent when the\n// user resumes it: the stall clock is still running from the old\n// session, so it reports as stalled on its first progress update.\n//\n// That outcome is pinned against plain values by \"a forgotten torrent\n// starts over\" in `engine-stall.js`. What this checks is the wiring:\n// the forgetting hangs off the torrent's own `close`, which WebTorrent\n// emits from `destroy()` whatever the reason, so no close path can skip\n// it. Driving a real stall from a spawned sidecar would take a minute\n// per case, so this is a source scan in the shape of\n// `engine-sweep-wiring.js`.\n\nconst ENTRY_SRC = path.join(\n  __dirname, '..', '..', 'src', 'engine', 'index.js')\n\nconst source = fs.readFileSync(ENTRY_SRC, 'utf8')\n\n// Every assertion below asserts PRESENCE, so comments are stripped\n// whole first, for the reason spelled out in `engine-sweep-wiring.js`.\nconst code = source\n  .replace(/\\/\\*[\\s\\S]*?\\*\\//g, '')\n  .split('\\n')\n  .filter((line) => !/^\\s*\\/\\//.test(line))\n  .join('\\n')\n\nconst wiring = /function addTorrentEvents \\(torrent\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n  .exec(code)\nconst events = wiring ? wiring[0] : ''\n\ntest('a closing torrent is handled where its events are wired',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(wiring, 'addTorrentEvents is found')\n    t.ok(/torrent\\.once?\\('close',\\s*torrentClose\\)/.test(events),\n      'the torrent\\'s own close event has a handler')\n    t.equal((code.match(/\\.once?\\('close',\\s*torrentClose\\)/g) || [])\n      .length, 1, 'and there is exactly one, so cleanup has one home')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the close handler forgets every kind of per-torrent state',\n  function (t) {\n    const handler = /function torrentClose \\(\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n {2}\\}/\n      .exec(events)\n    t.ok(handler, 'torrentClose is found inside addTorrentEvents')\n    const body = handler ? handler[0] : ''\n\n    t.ok(/const infoHash = torrent\\.infoHash/.test(body),\n      'it reads the closing torrent\\'s own info hash')\n    t.ok(/lastAnnounceAt\\.delete\\(infoHash\\)/.test(body),\n      'the announce time is forgotten')\n    t.ok(/corruptPeerStats\\.delete\\(infoHash\\)/.test(body),\n      'the corrupt-peer tally is forgotten')\n    t.ok(/stalls\\.forget\\(infoHash\\)/.test(body),\n      'and the stall samples are forgotten, so a resumed torrent ' +\n      'starts with a clean stall clock')\n\n    // WebTorrent destroys a duplicate add after giving it the live\n    // torrent's info hash, and removes a closing torrent from the\n    // client before emitting `close`. So a torrent still found under\n    // the hash at close time is someone else's, and its state stays.\n    const guardAt = body.search(/if \\(client\\.get\\(infoHash\\)\\) return/)\n    const forgetAt = body.search(/lastAnnounceAt\\.delete/)\n    t.ok(guardAt >= 0 && guardAt < forgetAt,\n      'a duplicate closing does not wipe the live torrent\\'s state')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('stop-torrenting relies on the close handler', function (t) {\n  const stop = /function stopTorrenting \\(infoHash\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n    .exec(code)\n  t.ok(stop, 'stopTorrenting is found')\n  const body = stop ? stop[0] : ''\n  t.ok(/torrent\\.destroy\\(\\)/.test(body),\n    'it destroys the torrent, which emits close')\n  t.notOk(/lastAnnounceAt\\.delete|corruptPeerStats\\.delete|stalls\\.forget/\n    .test(body), 'and does not keep a second copy of the cleanup')\n  t.end()\n})\n21:    \"test\": \"npm run lint && depcheck --ignores=standard,eslint,@babel/eslint-parser,madr,buffer,nightralph --ignore-dirs=build,build-vite,build-tauri,build-sidecar,dist && npm run test-unit\",\n22:    \"test-unit\": \"npm run build && tape test/unit/*.js\",\n23:    \"lint\": \"standard && npm run lint:boundary\",\n24:    \"lint:boundary\": \"eslint --no-eslintrc -c eslint-boundary.config.js src/renderer src/engine\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f96e20b8-4586-4652-a1ec-e1f2211c71ab","uuid":"76fc422e-a2a9-4d3e-9e3f-e0743c0f9ae8","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:20:21.858Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"AGENTS.md\nannounce.js\naudio-metadata.js\nbus-websocket.js\ndials.js\nhashfail.js\nindex.js\nstall.js\n# src/engine/\n\nThe torrent engine and its transport (ADR-012). Phase 3 moves the\nengine here from `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` and turns it into a Node\nsidecar; US-039 landed the transport seam first and US-040 added the\nsidecar entry point.\n\n**There is exactly one engine.** US-044 moved the UI onto the sidecar\nand US-045 deleted the hidden window, the engine that ran in it and the\n`ipcMain` relay between them. Engine behaviour has a single home now:\n`src/engine/index.js`.\n\n`build:main` compiles the whole of `src` except the four Vite-owned\npaths, so a file added here is emitted to `build/engine/` with no build\nchange -- that is also the path unit tests require.\n\n## The bus contract\n\nThe engine talks to the UI through an injected bus and never names a\ntransport API itself. A bus is exactly two functions:\n\n- `send(channel, ...args)` -- deliver one message to the UI.\n- `on(channel, handler)` -- subscribe; resolves to an unsubscribe\n  function. The handler receives the protocol's argument list only,\n  never the transport's own event object. Stripping that object is the\n  bus's job, not the caller's.\n\nThe bus carries no protocol. Channel names, argument lists and payload\nshapes live in the command and event tables in\n`docs/platform-shim.md`; the codec that turns them into bytes is\n`src/shared/wire.js`. A bus implementation that knows a channel name is\na bug.\n\nThe engine constructs its bus in exactly one place and\n`test/unit/engine-entry.js` asserts that, by scanning the engine source\nfor transport APIs, for stray `.send(` calls and for subscriptions that\nbypass `bus.on`. That scan is what keeps the seam substitutable, so keep\nit passing rather than relaxing it.\n\n`bus-electron.js`, the Phase 3 transitional implementation, is gone:\nUS-044 pointed the UI at `bus-websocket.js` and US-045 deleted the\nhidden window it depended on, along with its unit test.\n\nThis directory is outside `src/renderer/`, so the renderer's Node bans\ndo not apply: the sidecar is a Node process and uses `fs`, `crypto`\nand friends directly. Since US-046 both boundary mechanisms cover it\nfor the **opposite** mistake instead. `eslint-boundary.config.js` has\nan `src/engine/**` override banning `electron`, `@electron/remote` and\n`original-fs` plus every DOM global, and `npm run lint:boundary` lints\nthis tree as well as the renderer;\n`test/unit/platform-boundary.js` scans the same two things in source,\nwhich is what catches the `require()` spellings ESLint cannot see.\n\n## `bus-websocket.js`\n\nThe sidecar implementation, and the one that survives Phase 4. It binds\na `ws` server to `127.0.0.1` on port 0, encodes every frame with\n`src/shared/wire.js`, and exposes `send`/`on` plus three lifecycle\nfunctions the entry point needs: `listen()` (resolves to the bound\nport, idempotent), `address()` and `close()`.\n\nThree behaviours are contract, not incidental:\n\n- **A send with nobody connected is dropped, not queued.** Commands\n  always originate from the UI, so a connected client is the normal\n  case; the pre-connect queue that ADR-012 requires lives on the *UI*\n  side, in `ipc-engine.js`, and has since US-044.\n- **A malformed frame and a throwing handler are both reported through\n  `onError`, never rethrown and never fatal to the socket.** The engine\n  is a long-lived process; one bad message from a buggy UI must not end\n  the session. `index.js` wires `onError` to the same\n  `wt-uncaught-error` path the process handlers use.\n- **The token, when configured, is checked in `verifyClient`**, which\n  answers a bad upgrade with HTTP 401 before any frame is read. It is\n  read from `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_TOKEN` by `index.js`. No token means an\n  open server, which is only acceptable for running the sidecar by\n  hand. The `Origin` check ADR-012 also asks for is still not\n  implemented -- US-044 connected the UI without needing it, so it needs\n  the real header the Electron and Tauri webviews send and is US-046\n  territory.\n\n## `index.js`\n\nThe sidecar entry point: `node build/engine/index.js`. It imports no\nElectron module and uses no DOM.\n\n**The stdout contract with the host.** Exactly one line matches\n`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=<port>`, printed once the server is listening.\nThe host end is `src-tauri/src/engine.rs`, which holds the same prefix\nin `PORT_LINE_PREFIX`; keep the prefix and the `=` if you touch either\nside. Every other line on stdout and stderr is pumped through to the\nhost's own output under an `Engine: ` prefix. `src/main/` is gone, so\nthe Electron reader that older plans name here\n(`src/main/engine-process.js`, US-041) went with it.\n\nDifferences from the hidden-window engine, all deliberate:\n\n- No `ipcReadyWebTorrent`. Readiness is the port line plus an accepted\n  socket.\n- No `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and no `wt-poster`. Both needed a DOM;\n  US-043 deleted them from the wire and poster generation now runs in the\n  UI renderer (`src/renderer/lib/torrent-poster.js`, ADR-013). The engine\n  handles no media at all -- do not add a media dependency back to it.\n- No `window.client` and no `window.testOfflineMode`.\n- Uncaught errors come from `process.on('uncaughtException')` and\n  `process.on('unhandledRejection')` rather than a `window` error\n  listener, and still go out on `wt-uncaught-error`.\n- `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT` destroy the client and close every streaming\n  server, so a quit does not leave the download directory held open.\n- With `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE` set, stdin ending shuts the\n  process down the same way. That is the orphan backstop for a host\n  that dies without running its `before-quit`, and it is gated behind\n  the flag because a hand-run `node build/engine/index.js` gets\n  `/dev/null` on stdin, which ends immediately.\n- `getTorrent()` throws a plain `Error` with the same message\n  `TorrentKeyNotFoundError` produced. The class lives in\n  `src/renderer/lib/errors.js`, only its message ever crossed the wire,\n  and the sidecar does not import renderer code.\n\nThe streaming servers are a `Map` keyed by info hash, and each entry\ncounts its holders (US-043): `wt-start-server` is idempotent per torrent\nand always replies, `wt-stop-server` takes the info hash to release, and\nthe server dies with its last holder. Poster generation and playback can\nhold servers for different torrents at once, so a single module-level\n`server` is not enough any more.\n\n`config.TORRENT_PATH` and `config.POSTER_PATH` resolve under plain\n`node` because US-040 made `src/config.js` require `electron` lazily,\ninside the main-process branch of `getPath()`. Do not hoist that back\nto a top-level import: the packaged sidecar has no\n`node_modules/electron` to resolve.\n\nThe host runs the sidecar as **Electron in Node mode**\n(`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1`), not as a separate `node` binary, so that the\npackaged app needs no second runtime. In that mode\n`process.versions.electron` is set but `process.type` is not and\n`require('electron')` has no `app`, which is why `getPath()` in\n`src/config.js` branches on `process.type` and treats anything that is\nneither `'renderer'` nor `'browser'` as a plain Node process. A check\nfor \"is Electron present\" there breaks the sidecar at load, and only\noutside `NODE_ENV=test` (portable config skips the branch).\n\n## Per-torrent state and the torrent's `close`\n\nState the engine keys by info hash (`lastAnnounceAt`,\n`corruptPeerStats`, the stall tracker) is forgotten in one place:\n`torrentClose`, the `close` handler `addTorrentEvents` installs.\nWebTorrent emits `close` from `destroy()` whatever the cause --\n`wt-stop-torrenting`, removal, a fatal error -- so `stopTorrenting`\nonly destroys and does not repeat the cleanup. Add new per-torrent\n134:  bus.on('wt-start-torrenting', (torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections) =>\n135:    startTorrenting(torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections))\n148:  bus.on('wt-select-files', (infoHash, selections) =>\n149:    selectFiles(infoHash, selections))\n222:function startTorrenting (torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections) {\n235:  torrent.once('ready', () => selectFiles(torrent, selections))\n240:  // The per-torrent state goes in `torrentClose`, which `destroy()`\n329:  torrent.once('close', torrentClose)\n343:  function torrentClose () {\n672:function selectFiles (torrentOrInfoHash, selections) {\n681:    throw new Error('selectFiles: missing torrent ' + torrentOrInfoHash)\n686:  // selection with individual selections for each file, so we can\n687:  // select/deselect files later on\n688:  if (!selections) {\n689:    selections = new Array(torrent.files.length).fill(true)\n693:  if (selections.length !== torrent.files.length) {\n694:    throw new Error('got ' + selections.length + ' file selections, ' +\n698:  // Remove default selection (whole torrent)\n699:  torrent.deselect(0, torrent.pieces.length - 1, false)\n701:  // Add selections (individual files)\n702:  selections.forEach((selection, i) => {\n704:    if (selection) {\n705:      file.select()\n707:      console.log('deselecting file ' + i + ' of torrent ' + torrent.name)\n708:      file.deselect()\nconst test = require('tape')\nconst fs = require('fs')\nconst path = require('path')\n\n// The engine keeps per-torrent diagnostic state -- announce times,\n// corrupt-peer tallies, stall samples -- keyed by info hash. A torrent\n// can close without `wt-stop-torrenting` (a fatal error, say), and\n// state left behind under its info hash greets the torrent when the\n// user resumes it: the stall clock is still running from the old\n// session, so it reports as stalled on its first progress update.\n//\n// That outcome is pinned against plain values by \"a forgotten torrent\n// starts over\" in `engine-stall.js`. What this checks is the wiring:\n// the forgetting hangs off the torrent's own `close`, which WebTorrent\n// emits from `destroy()` whatever the reason, so no close path can skip\n// it. Driving a real stall from a spawned sidecar would take a minute\n// per case, so this is a source scan in the shape of\n// `engine-sweep-wiring.js`.\n\nconst ENTRY_SRC = path.join(\n  __dirname, '..', '..', 'src', 'engine', 'index.js')\n\nconst source = fs.readFileSync(ENTRY_SRC, 'utf8')\n\n// Every assertion below asserts PRESENCE, so comments are stripped\n// whole first, for the reason spelled out in `engine-sweep-wiring.js`.\nconst code = source\n  .replace(/\\/\\*[\\s\\S]*?\\*\\//g, '')\n  .split('\\n')\n  .filter((line) => !/^\\s*\\/\\//.test(line))\n  .join('\\n')\n\nconst wiring = /function addTorrentEvents \\(torrent\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n  .exec(code)\nconst events = wiring ? wiring[0] : ''\n\ntest('a closing torrent is handled where its events are wired',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(wiring, 'addTorrentEvents is found')\n    t.ok(/torrent\\.once?\\('close',\\s*torrentClose\\)/.test(events),\n      'the torrent\\'s own close event has a handler')\n    t.equal((code.match(/\\.once?\\('close',\\s*torrentClose\\)/g) || [])\n      .length, 1, 'and there is exactly one, so cleanup has one home')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the close handler forgets every kind of per-torrent state',\n  function (t) {\n    const handler = /function torrentClose \\(\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n {2}\\}/\n      .exec(events)\n    t.ok(handler, 'torrentClose is found inside addTorrentEvents')\n    const body = handler ? handler[0] : ''\n\n    t.ok(/const infoHash = torrent\\.infoHash/.test(body),\n      'it reads the closing torrent\\'s own info hash')\n    t.ok(/lastAnnounceAt\\.delete\\(infoHash\\)/.test(body),\n      'the announce time is forgotten')\n    t.ok(/corruptPeerStats\\.delete\\(infoHash\\)/.test(body),\n      'the corrupt-peer tally is forgotten')\n    t.ok(/stalls\\.forget\\(infoHash\\)/.test(body),\n      'and the stall samples are forgotten, so a resumed torrent ' +\n      'starts with a clean stall clock')\n\n    // WebTorrent destroys a duplicate add after giving it the live\n    // torrent's info hash, and removes a closing torrent from the\n    // client before emitting `close`. So a torrent still found under\n    // the hash at close time is someone else's, and its state stays.\n    const guardAt = body.search(/if \\(client\\.get\\(infoHash\\)\\) return/)\n    const forgetAt = body.search(/lastAnnounceAt\\.delete/)\n    t.ok(guardAt >= 0 && guardAt < forgetAt,\n      'a duplicate closing does not wipe the live torrent\\'s state')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('stop-torrenting relies on the close handler', function (t) {\n  const stop = /function stopTorrenting \\(infoHash\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n    .exec(code)\n  t.ok(stop, 'stopTorrenting is found')\n  const body = stop ? stop[0] : ''\n  t.ok(/torrent\\.destroy\\(\\)/.test(body),\n    'it destroys the torrent, which emits close')\n  t.notOk(/lastAnnounceAt\\.delete|corruptPeerStats\\.delete|stalls\\.forget/\n    .test(body), 'and does not keep a second copy of the cleanup')\n  t.end()\n})\n21:    \"test\": \"npm run lint && depcheck --ignores=standard,eslint,@babel/eslint-parser,madr,buffer,nightralph --ignore-dirs=build,build-vite,build-tauri,build-sidecar,dist && npm run test-unit\",\n22:    \"test-unit\": \"npm run build && tape test/unit/*.js\",\n23:    \"lint\": \"standard && npm run lint:boundary\",\n24:    \"lint:boundary\": \"eslint --no-eslintrc -c eslint-boundary.config.js src/renderer src/engine\",","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WYt3A2fnXnvbuZr2vdnFXU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg8pmAa34LvXYAWqdzhow","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016X5bvM7XRtGVmztC4Sh5Mc","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/nick/code/webtorrent-desktop.memory-leaks-05-file-selection-applies-only-changes; sed -n 150,400p src/engine/AGENTS.md; sed -n 1,60p src/engine/index.js; sed -n 215,250p src/engine/index.js; sed -n 320,375p src/engine/index.js; sed -n 665,720p src/engine/index.js; cat src/engine/stall.js | head -60; head -40 test/unit/engine-stall.js"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5924,"cache_read_input_tokens":45996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5924},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f96e20b8-4586-4652-a1ec-e1f2211c71ab","uuid":"fd574d66-4176-4756-b1d7-a0c21cfc6dfa","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:20:29.924Z","request_id":"req_011Cg8pmALPpd1E2hoZEgw2c","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016X5bvM7XRtGVmztC4Sh5Mc":{"command":"cd /Users/nick/code/webtorrent-desktop.memory-leaks-05-file-selection-applies-only-changes; sed -n 150,400p src/engine/AGENTS.md; sed -n 1,60p src/engine/index.js; sed -n 215,250p src/engine/index.js; sed -n 320,375p src/engine/index.js; sed -n 665,720p src/engine/index.js; cat src/engine/stall.js | head -60; head -40 test/unit/engine-stall.js"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016X5bvM7XRtGVmztC4Sh5Mc","type":"tool_result","content":"only destroys and does not repeat the cleanup. Add new per-torrent\ncleanup to that handler, not to `stopTorrenting`.\n\nThe handler returns early when `client.get(infoHash)` still finds a\ntorrent. WebTorrent removes a closing torrent from the client before\nemitting `close`, so a hit means a duplicate add is being destroyed\nunder the live torrent's hash, and that state is not its to forget.\n\n`test/unit/engine-torrent-close-wiring.js` pins this by source scan.\n\n## `announce.js`\n\n`announceNow(torrent, dht)` is the discovery half of \"destroy and\nre-add\": a tracker `update()` plus a DHT `lookup()`, with no re-verify\nof pieces on disk and no dropped peer connections. It exists because\nthe natural cadence is slow -- 30 minutes per tracker, 15 minutes plus\njitter for the DHT -- so a torrent whose last peer drops can sit at\nzero peers for half an hour.\n\nThe sweep automates it. Once a minute `index.js` calls\n`runAnnounceSweep`, which re-announces every torrent that has zero\npeers, is not finished and is not paused. Three guards keep it from\nbeing worse than the bug it fixes, and they are the reason the logic\nlives here rather than inline in `index.js`, where it could not be\ntested: a five minute per-torrent cooldown, comfortably above any\ntracker's `min interval`; a cap of five torrents per sweep, served\nleast-recently-announced first so a long list goes round robin; and one\nsummary line per sweep that did anything, carrying the deferred count,\nbecause a silent truncation reads as \"nothing else needed it\".\n\nBe precise about that last one, because the obvious reading is too\nnarrow. The line is not reserved for capped sweeps: a sweep that\nannounces one torrent and defers nothing still logs. What is silent is\na sweep with nothing to do -- `runAnnounceSweep` returns early only\nwhen it selected nothing *and* deferred nothing, so an idle engine\nsays nothing at all.\n\n`index.js` owns only the timer and the `lastAnnounceAt` map. The map\nis seeded when a torrent's info hash arrives -- `client.add()` has just\nannounced `started` and a second announce a minute later would be waste\n-- and cleared when the torrent closes (see above).\n\nThe three tunables are exported from here, not from `index.js`:\n`SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`, `COOLDOWN_MS` and `MAX_PER_SWEEP`. `index.js`\nimports the interval rather than repeating `60 * 1000`, so the cadence\nhas one home. `selectStalled` is exported next to `runAnnounceSweep`\nbecause the guards are worth testing without announcing anything.\n\n`test/unit/engine-sweep-wiring.js` pins that wiring by scanning the\n`index.js` source: both imports, `setInterval(runSweep,\nSWEEP_INTERVAL_MS)`, and the seed and the delete on `lastAnnounceAt`.\nAn interval that fires once a minute is too slow to drive from a\nspawned sidecar, and a test-only override of it would be production\ncode that exists for a test -- so renaming `runSweep` or inlining the\nconstant fails that scan instead of going unnoticed.\n\n## `audio-metadata.js`\n\n`readAudioMetadata(file, torrentPath, options, parser)` is the only\nplace the engine hands `music-metadata` a file. An unfinished file is\nparsed from `file.createReadStream()`, and that stream holds a piece\nbuffer and a high-priority selection until it is destroyed -- the\nlibrary stops reading once it has what it needs but never destroys the\nstream it was given. So the module owns the stream and destroys it once\nthe parse settles, either way. Finished files go through `parseFile`,\nwhich closes its own file. Do not call `mm.parseStream` from `index.js`\nagain: `test/unit/engine-audio-metadata-wiring.js` scans for exactly\nthat. `parser` defaults to `music-metadata` and is a parameter so\n`test/unit/engine-audio-metadata.js` can drive a real `Readable`\nwithout audio.\n// The torrent engine, as a standalone Node process (US-040, ADR-012).\n//\n// This is the sidecar entry point: `node build/engine/index.js`. It\n// imports no Electron module and touches no DOM. It binds a WebSocket\n// server to 127.0.0.1 on a kernel-assigned port, prints that port on\n// stdout as one line, and then speaks exactly the `wt-*` protocol in\n// the command and event tables of `docs/platform-shim.md`.\n//\n// Until US-045 cuts the app over, the live engine is still\n// `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` in the hidden window. The two are\n// deliberately separate files rather than one shared core: keeping the\n// live engine byte-identical is worth more during the move than\n// avoiding the duplication, which US-045 deletes. Any change to engine\n// behaviour before then has to land in both.\n//\n// Two channels of the hidden-window engine are gone here:\n//\n// - `ipcReadyWebTorrent`, the `ipcMain` relay's handshake. Readiness is\n//   now the port line plus an accepted socket.\n// - `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and `wt-poster`, which needed a DOM.\n//   US-043 deleted both from the wire: poster generation runs in the UI\n//   renderer now (ADR-013), and the engine handles no media at all.\n\nimport crypto from 'crypto'\nimport fs from 'fs'\nimport path from 'path'\nimport util from 'util'\nimport networkAddress from 'network-address'\nimport WebTorrent from 'webtorrent'\n\nimport config from '../config'\nimport { runAnnounceSweep, SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS } from './announce'\nimport { readAudioMetadata } from './audio-metadata'\nimport { createWebSocketBus } from './bus-websocket'\nimport { limitPendingDials } from './dials'\nimport {\n  bannedSinceProgress, createHashFailTracker, guardIncomingPeers,\n  guardPeerAdds, parseFailedPiece\n} from './hashfail'\nimport { createStallTracker, verifiedBytes } from './stall'\n\nconsole.time('init')\n\n// The one line US-041's supervisor parses out of the child's stdout.\n// Everything else the engine prints is log output.\nconst PORT_LINE_PREFIX = 'WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT='\n\n/**\n * Version number in Azureus-style. Generated from major and minor semver\n * version. For example:\n *   '0.16.1' -> '0016'\n *   '1.2.5' -> '0102'\n */\nconst VERSION_STR = config.APP_VERSION\n  .replace(/\\d*./g, v => `0${v % 100}`.slice(-2))\n  .slice(0, 4)\n\n/**\n * Version prefix string (used in peer ID). WebTorrent uses the\n * Azureus-style encoding: '-', two characters for client id ('WW'), four\n// Sets the default trackers\nfunction setGlobalTrackers (globalTrackers) {\n  globalThis.WEBTORRENT_ANNOUNCE = globalTrackers\n}\n\n// Starts a given TorrentID, which can be an infohash, magnet URI, etc.\n// Returns a WebTorrent object. See https://git.io/vik9M\nfunction startTorrenting (torrentKey, torrentID, path, fileModtimes, selections) {\n  console.log('starting torrent %s: %s', torrentKey, torrentID)\n\n  const torrent = client.add(torrentID, {\n    path,\n    fileModtimes\n  })\n  torrent.key = torrentKey\n\n  // Listen for ready event, progress notifications, etc\n  addTorrentEvents(torrent)\n\n  // Only download the files the user wants, not necessarily all files\n  torrent.once('ready', () => selectFiles(torrent, selections))\n}\n\nfunction stopTorrenting (infoHash) {\n  console.log('--- STOP TORRENTING: ', infoHash)\n  // The per-torrent state goes in `torrentClose`, which `destroy()`\n  // triggers, so this path and a fatal error share one cleanup.\n  const torrent = client.get(infoHash)\n  if (torrent) torrent.destroy()\n}\n\n// Ask any torrent sitting at zero peers to re-announce. Without this a\n// torrent whose last peer drops waits out the tracker's own interval,\n// which `bittorrent-tracker` defaults to 30 minutes for HTTP and UDP,\n// or the DHT's 15 minutes plus jitter. The guards -- who qualifies, a\n// five minute per-torrent cooldown, a cap per sweep -- are in\n  torrent.on('infoHash', () => {\n    // `client.add()` announces `started` as it goes, so the sweep's\n    // clock for this torrent starts here rather than at zero.\n    lastAnnounceAt.set(torrent.infoHash, Date.now())\n    bus.send('wt-parsed', torrent.key, torrent.infoHash, torrent.magnetURI)\n  })\n  torrent.on('metadata', torrentMetadata)\n  torrent.on('ready', torrentReady)\n  torrent.on('done', torrentDone)\n  torrent.once('close', torrentClose)\n\n  // The single place a closing torrent is forgotten. WebTorrent emits\n  // `close` from `destroy()` whatever the cause -- stop-torrenting,\n  // removal, a fatal error -- so state keyed by this info hash cannot\n  // outlive the torrent and greet a resumed one. Stale stall samples\n  // were the visible symptom: a resumed torrent reported as stalled\n  // on its first progress update.\n  //\n  // WebTorrent takes a closing torrent off the client before emitting\n  // `close`, so a torrent still found under this hash is a different,\n  // live one. That happens when a duplicate add is destroyed: it was\n  // given the live torrent's info hash first, and the state is not its\n  // to forget.\n  function torrentClose () {\n    const infoHash = torrent.infoHash\n    if (client.get(infoHash)) return\n    lastAnnounceAt.delete(infoHash)\n    corruptPeerStats.delete(infoHash)\n    stalls.forget(infoHash)\n  }\n\n  function torrentMetadata () {\n    const info = getTorrentInfo(torrent)\n    bus.send('wt-metadata', torrent.key, info)\n\n    updateTorrentProgress()\n  }\n\n  function torrentReady () {\n    const info = getTorrentInfo(torrent)\n    bus.send('wt-ready', torrent.key, info)\n    bus.send('wt-ready-' + torrent.infoHash, torrent.key, info)\n\n    updateTorrentProgress()\n  }\n\n  function torrentDone () {\n    const info = getTorrentInfo(torrent)\n    bus.send('wt-done', torrent.key, info)\n\n    updateTorrentProgress()\n\n    torrent.getFileModtimes((err, fileModtimes) => {\n      if (err) return onError(err)\n      bus.send('wt-file-modtimes', torrent.key, fileModtimes)\n    })\n          `error getting audio metadata for ${infoHash}:${index}`,\n          err\n        )\n      }\n    )\n}\n\nfunction selectFiles (torrentOrInfoHash, selections) {\n  // Get the torrent object\n  let torrent\n  if (typeof torrentOrInfoHash === 'string') {\n    torrent = client.get(torrentOrInfoHash)\n  } else {\n    torrent = torrentOrInfoHash\n  }\n  if (!torrent) {\n    throw new Error('selectFiles: missing torrent ' + torrentOrInfoHash)\n  }\n\n  // Selections not specified?\n  // Load all files. We still need to replace the default whole-torrent\n  // selection with individual selections for each file, so we can\n  // select/deselect files later on\n  if (!selections) {\n    selections = new Array(torrent.files.length).fill(true)\n  }\n\n  // Selections specified incorrectly?\n  if (selections.length !== torrent.files.length) {\n    throw new Error('got ' + selections.length + ' file selections, ' +\n      'but the torrent contains ' + torrent.files.length + ' files')\n  }\n\n  // Remove default selection (whole torrent)\n  torrent.deselect(0, torrent.pieces.length - 1, false)\n\n  // Add selections (individual files)\n  selections.forEach((selection, i) => {\n    const file = torrent.files[i]\n    if (selection) {\n      file.select()\n    } else {\n      console.log('deselecting file ' + i + ' of torrent ' + torrent.name)\n      file.deselect()\n    }\n  })\n}\n\n// Gets a WebTorrent handle by torrentKey\n// Throws an Error if we're not currently torrenting anything w/ that key\n//\n// The hidden-window engine threw `TorrentKeyNotFoundError` from\n// `src/renderer/lib/errors.js`. The class identity never crossed the\n// wire -- only the message did -- and the sidecar does not import\n// renderer code, so the message is what it keeps.\nfunction getTorrent (torrentKey) {\n// Progress measured in verified bytes, and the stall that falls out of\n// it.\n//\n// The two numbers WebTorrent hands the UI both answer a different\n// question than the one the UI is asking:\n//\n// - `torrent.downloadSpeed` is fed from the raw socket\n//   (`lib/peer.js:270`), so it counts handshakes, keepalives, `have`\n//   messages and every other byte a peer sends. A peer that never\n//   serves a usable block still shows tens of bytes per second.\n// - `torrent.downloaded` deliberately includes in-progress piece\n//   buffers (`lib/torrent.js:147`), so it climbs as a piece fills and\n//   drops back when that piece fails to verify.\n//\n// Dividing what is missing by the first is how a torrent receiving\n// nothing usable reported \"12 h 25 min remaining\", and watching the\n// second is how a torrent whose pieces never verify looks like it is\n// making progress.\n//\n// So the two questions are answered from two different counters, which\n// is not the same as answering both from the bitfield:\n//\n// - **How fast is it going**, for the ETA, is bitfield bytes. An\n//   estimate may only be built on data that is certainly kept.\n// - **Has it stopped**, for the status line, is the high-water mark of\n//   `torrent.downloaded`.\n//\n// The second used to be the bitfield too, and that was wrong in a way\n// that took measuring to see. Pieces verify in lumps: at 8 KB/s with\n// megabyte pieces one lands every few minutes, so a torrent that was\n// downloading perfectly well went a minute without verifying anything\n// and got labelled \"Stalled\". Measured over four minutes, a torrent\n// gaining 2 MB verified something in 13% of ticks and one gaining\n// 180 KB in none of them -- both were reported stuck the whole time.\n//\n// The high-water mark is what makes `downloaded` usable for this\n// despite the sawtooth in the header above. A piece that fails takes\n// the counter back down and the next attempt climbs the same ground\n// again, so the *maximum* only moves when the torrent holds payload it\n// has never held before. The corrupt-peer case this module was written\n// for still reads as stalled -- the first attempt sets the mark and\n// every retry after it is flat -- while a slow download moves the mark\n// every 16 KB block and reads as what it is.\n//\n// Everything here is a parameter, including the clock, so the policy\n// can be tested without waiting on it.\n\n// How long a torrent may go without verifying a single byte before the\n// UI calls it stalled. Long enough that ordinary peer churn -- a seed\n// dropping, a slow piece, a tracker interval -- does not flicker the\n// label, short enough to be useful.\nexport const STALL_AFTER_MS = 60000\n\n// The window verified speed is averaged over. Pieces land in lumps,\n// often a megabyte at a time and seconds apart, so a one-second sample\n// reads as zero through most of a healthy download. Averaging over a\n// window turns those lumps back into a rate an ETA can use.\nexport const SPEED_WINDOW_MS = 30000\n\n/**\nconst test = require('tape')\n\n// Progress measured in verified bytes, the stall measured in payload\n// bytes, and the reasons they are not the same counter. The clock is a\n// parameter, like the sweep's in `announce.js`, so none of this waits\n// on real time.\nconst {\n  createStallTracker, verifiedBytes, STALL_AFTER_MS, SPEED_WINDOW_MS\n} = require('../../build/engine/stall')\n\nconst KEY = 'ccf09fcb'\nconst MB = 1024 * 1024\n\n// Both counters at the same figure, for the cases where the distinction\n// between them is not what is under test.\nconst both = (bytes) => ({ verified: bytes, payload: bytes })\n\ntest('the first sample has nothing to compare against', function (t) {\n  const tracker = createStallTracker()\n  const first = tracker.sample(KEY, both(100 * MB), 1000)\n\n  t.equal(first.verifiedSpeed, 0, 'no speed can be derived from one point')\n  t.equal(first.stalledForMs, 0, 'and nothing has stalled yet')\n  t.equal(first.stalled, false, 'so it is not reported as stalled')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('speed is verified bytes over elapsed time', function (t) {\n  const tracker = createStallTracker()\n  tracker.sample(KEY, both(0), 0)\n  const result = tracker.sample(KEY, both(10 * MB), 10000)\n\n  t.equal(result.verifiedSpeed, MB, '10 MB verified over 10s is 1 MB/s')\n  t.equal(result.stalled, false, 'and it is plainly moving')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The bug this whole module exists for. A peer that sends handshakes,\n// keepalives and `have` messages but never a usable block keeps\n// `torrent.downloadSpeed` off zero, because that counts every byte off","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f96e20b8-4586-4652-a1ec-e1f2211c71ab","uuid":"7f3c30d7-bba4-4ef1-9ef7-c348e4a912e5","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:20:30.112Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"only destroys and does not repeat the cleanup. Add new per-torrent\ncleanup to that handler, not to `stopTorrenting`.\n\nThe handler returns early when `client.get(infoHash)` still finds a\ntorrent. WebTorrent removes a closing torrent from the client before\nemitting `close`, so a hit means a duplicate add is being destroyed\nunder the live torrent's hash, and that state is not its to forget.\n\n`test/unit/engine-torrent-close-wiring.js` pins this by source scan.\n\n## `announce.js`\n\n`announceNow(torrent, dht)` is the discovery half of \"destroy and\nre-add\": a tracker `update()` plus a DHT `lookup()`, with no re-verify\nof pieces on disk and no dropped peer connections. It exists because\nthe natural cadence is slow -- 30 minutes per tracker, 15 minutes plus\njitter for the DHT -- so a torrent whose last peer drops can sit at\nzero peers for half an hour.\n\nThe sweep automates it. Once a minute `index.js` calls\n`runAnnounceSweep`, which re-announces every torrent that has zero\npeers, is not finished and is not paused. Three guards keep it from\nbeing worse than the bug it fixes, and they are the reason the logic\nlives here rather than inline in `index.js`, where it could not be\ntested: a five minute per-torrent cooldown, comfortably above any\ntracker's `min interval`; a cap of five torrents per sweep, served\nleast-recently-announced first so a long list goes round robin; and one\nsummary line per sweep that did anything, carrying the deferred count,\nbecause a silent truncation reads as \"nothing else needed it\".\n\nBe precise about that last one, because the obvious reading is too\nnarrow. The line is not reserved for capped sweeps: a sweep that\nannounces one torrent and defers nothing still logs. What is silent is\na sweep with nothing to do -- `runAnnounceSweep` returns early only\nwhen it selected nothing *and* deferred nothing, so an idle engine\nsays nothing at all.\n\n`index.js` owns only the timer and the `lastAnnounceAt` map. The map\nis seeded when a torrent's info hash arrives -- `client.add()` has just\nannounced `started` and a second announce a minute later would be waste\n-- and cleared when the torrent closes (see above).\n\nThe three tunables are exported from here, not from `index.js`:\n`SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`, `COOLDOWN_MS` and `MAX_PER_SWEEP`. `index.js`\nimports the interval rather than repeating `60 * 1000`, so the cadence\nhas one home. `selectStalled` is exported next to `runAnnounceSweep`\nbecause the guards are worth testing without announcing anything.\n\n`test/unit/engine-sweep-wiring.js` pins that wiring by scanning the\n`index.js` source: both imports, `setInterval(runSweep,\nSWEEP_INTERVAL_MS)`, and the seed and the delete on `lastAnnounceAt`.\nAn interval that fires once a minute is too slow to drive from a\nspawned sidecar, and a test-only override of it would be production\ncode that exists for a test -- so renaming `runSweep` or inlining the\nconstant fails that scan instead of going unnoticed.\n\n## `audio-metadata.js`\n\n`readAudioMetadata(file, torrentPath, options, parser)` is the only\nplace the engine hands `music-metadata` a file. An unfinished file is\nparsed from `file.createReadStream()`, and that stream holds a piece\nbuffer and a high-priority selection until it is destroyed -- the\nlibrary stops reading once it has what it needs but never destroys the\nstream it was given. So the module owns the stream and destroys it once\nthe parse settles, either way. Finished files go through `parseFile`,\nwhich closes its own file. Do not call `mm.parseStream` from `index.js`\nagain: `test/unit/engine-audio-metadata-wiring.js` scans for exactly\nthat. `parser` defaults to `music-metadata` and is a parameter so\n`test/unit/engine-audio-metadata.js` can drive a real `Readable`\nwithout audio.\n// The torrent engine, as a standalone Node process (US-040, ADR-012).\n//\n// This is the sidecar entry point: `node build/engine/index.js`. It\n// imports no Electron module and touches no DOM. It binds a WebSocket\n// server to 127.0.0.1 on a kernel-assigned port, prints that port on\n// stdout as one line, and then speaks exactly the `wt-*` protocol in\n// the command and event tables of `docs/platform-shim.md`.\n//\n// Until US-045 cuts the app over, the live engine is still\n// `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` in the hidden window. The two are\n// deliberately separate files rather than one shared core: keeping the\n// live engine byte-identical is worth more during the move than\n// avoiding the duplication, which US-045 deletes. Any change to engine\n// behaviour before then has to land in both.\n//\n// Two channels of the hidden-window engine are gone here:\n//\n// - `ipcReadyWebTorrent`, the `ipcMain` relay's handshake. Readiness is\n//   now the port line plus an accepted socket.\n// - `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and `wt-poster`, which needed a DOM.\n//   US-043 deleted both from the wire: poster generation runs in the UI\n//   renderer now (ADR-013), and the engine handles no media at all.\n\nimport crypto from 'crypto'\nimport fs from 'fs'\nimport path from 'path'\nimport util from 'util'\nimport networkAddress from 'network-address'\nimport WebTorrent from 'webtorrent'\n\nimport config from '../config'\nimport { runAnnounceSweep, SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS } from './announce'\nimport { readAudioMetadata } from './audio-metadata'\nimport { createWebSocketBus } from './bus-websocket'\nimport { limitPendingDials } from '.